"""Original Quran Hub editorial copy. Service promises are deliberately bounded."""

FOCUS = {
 'beginner':('Finding the right first reading exercise','Noorani Qaida','noorani-qaida', ['Recognise a few individual Arabic letters without help','Try the vowel sounds the teacher demonstrates','Attempt one joined word at a comfortable pace'], 'A starting assessment should identify a teachable next step. Knowing a letter name, producing its sound and reading it inside a joined word are different skills. Ask the tutor to explain which of these is secure before recommending a faster reading programme.'),
 'reading':('Turning familiar recitation into independent reading','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Read a short familiar passage','Try a suitable unfamiliar line','Identify where you rely on a prompt rather than the written word'],'A familiar surah can conceal a reading gap because its sound is already remembered. A useful assessment makes room for both familiar recitation and an appropriate new exercise, then selects practice that helps the learner follow written text independently.'),
 'pronunciation':('Making recitation correction specific','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Read a passage containing a difficult sound','Listen to the tutor model the distinction','Repeat the sound inside a word and a short phrase'],'Good correction explains what changes and lets the learner try again. Ask the tutor to limit the first task to a manageable sound or rule, rather than interrupt every word with several unfamiliar terms. Detailed advanced Tajweed should follow a suitable reading foundation.'),
 'adult':('Private Quran learning for an adult starting point','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Explain your current reading experience privately','Choose a manageable passage or first-letter exercise','Agree one goal that fits your actual working week'],'An adult course should respect both the learner’s starting point and their time. Being older does not mean the first lesson must skip basic letters; reading confidently also does not mean the teacher should overlook recurring mistakes. Judge the explanation, not a promise of unusually fast completion.'),
 'siblings':('Organising individual attention for a family','Noorani Qaida','noorani-qaida',['Give the starting level of each learner','Discuss whether consecutive slots are practical','Ask for a separate next-step recommendation for each child'],'A convenient family timetable is useful only if each learner still receives appropriate feedback. Compare ages, current reading and attention separately. Confirm how individual sessions and fees will work before assuming that one booking or one course plan covers everyone.'),
 'shift':('Agreeing a routine around changing availability','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Share an ordinary week and an unusually busy week','Offer a preferred UK-time window and a backup','Confirm how changes and missed sessions are handled'],'A timetable should be agreed before it is treated as a booking. Explain unavoidable commitments and ask for the attendance arrangement in writing. Availability can change, and the word flexible does not itself define cancellation, credits, refunds or replacement lessons.'),
 'student':('Keeping Quran study sustainable alongside other learning','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Describe the weeks in which study demands increase','Choose a minimum practice task that remains realistic','Discuss changes before an assessment or term break'],'Protecting continuity does not require promising the same workload every week. Use the teacher’s time for feedback you cannot reliably give yourself, then keep independent work focused. A timetable change and a change in home practice are separate decisions that should be discussed clearly.'),
 'connection':('Testing whether the online format works at home','Noorani Qaida','noorani-qaida',['Join from the device and room you will normally use','Check the shared page and two-way audio','Try a correction and explain what you heard'],'A connection that opens a call is not the same as a connection that supports careful recitation. Clear sound, a readable page and the learner’s comfort all matter. Use the trial to compare the actual experience with another format, including in-person tuition where that is more suitable.'),
 'commute':('Choosing a lesson window the household can keep','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Allow a realistic transition after school or work','Compare a weekday window with a weekend alternative','Test the learner’s attention at the proposed time'],'Removing travel does not automatically create useful study time. A learner who is hungry, tired or rushing between activities may need a different slot. Agree a practical routine with the tutor instead of filling the first gap in a calendar and hoping it remains workable.'),
 'shortpractice':('Making a small home task useful','Noorani Qaida','noorani-qaida',['Ask for one clearly demonstrated exercise','Check that the learner can explain the task','Bring one uncertainty back to the next lesson'],'Home practice should reinforce what the teacher has already demonstrated. A short, accurate exercise can be easier to repeat than an ambitious page target. Parents do not need to introduce new Arabic rules or guess at pronunciation corrections to support a dependable routine.'),
 'transfer':('Continuing after another teacher or beginner book','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Bring the last book or lesson number used','Show one secure skill and one difficult example','Ask what should continue and what needs revision'],'A change of teacher should begin with listening. Books and teaching orders can differ, and remembered terminology does not always show what can be applied independently. Ask the tutor to explain the connection between earlier work and the next exercise before committing to a restart or a rapid move ahead.'),
 'goals':('Choosing one priority before adding more subjects','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Describe the result you want in ordinary language','Show the reading skill that supports that goal','Ask which course should come first'],'Reading, memorisation and understanding can support each other, but a beginner does not need to pursue all three at the same intensity. Choose a first objective that can be assessed, then review it as skills develop. A named course should explain the path, not replace a level check.'),
 'progress':('Looking for evidence beyond a completed page','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Ask which skill is being developed','Listen to a short example before and after practice','Check whether the skill transfers to different text'],'Progress can include clearer letter sounds, less prompting or more accurate use of a rule. Ask for a plain-language explanation and a suitable example. Completing pages quickly is not enough if the same error remains or the learner cannot apply the skill independently.'),
 'hifz':('Balancing new memorisation with retention','Quran memorisation (Hifz)','quran-memorization-hifz',['Read accurately before agreeing a new target','Explain time available for revision outside class','Ask how recent and older memorisation will be reviewed'],'A Hifz plan needs room for retention as well as new verses. Terms such as sabaq, sabqi and manzil may be used differently between teaching systems, so ask what the actual revision routine means. A smaller target maintained accurately can be more useful than a large quantity that cannot be reviewed.'),
 'returning':('Restarting with the skills you still retain','Quran reading with Tajweed','quran-reading-tajweed',['Start with a familiar short passage','Try an appropriate unfamiliar reading exercise','Identify one foundation to refresh first'],'Time away from study does not erase every skill, and remembered recitation does not guarantee independent reading. A trial should identify both strengths and gaps. Ask for a starting task that rebuilds accuracy without turning the first lesson into a test of confidence.'),
}

COURSES = [
 dict(slug='noorani-qaida',name='Noorani Qaida',title='Learn Noorani Qaida Online: A Clear Start for Beginners',level='Beginner',image='2026/10/qh-uae-course-noorani-qaida.webp',intro='Start with the Arabic alphabet, vowel sounds and joined words in a one-to-one lesson. Noorani Qaida is a foundation course for learners who cannot yet decode Quranic text reliably, including children, adults and new Muslims.',topics=['Recognising letters in their different shapes','Distinguishing the sounds modelled by the tutor','Reading short vowels and joined words','Applying introductory reading rules in an appropriate order'],path=['Check recognition and listening before setting a reading target.','Practise a small group of sounds accurately, then read them in connected examples.','Review a new example before moving forward so recognition becomes independent reading.'],mistake='Do not confuse naming a letter with producing its sound accurately. Repeating an entire page without feedback can reinforce a mistake; bring the uncertain sound back to the teacher.',next='quran-reading-tajweed'),
 dict(slug='quran-reading-tajweed',name='Quran Reading with Tajweed',title='Online Quran Reading with Tajweed for Clearer Recitation',level='Foundation to intermediate',image='2026/08/course-tajweed-reading.webp',intro='Develop reading fluency while a tutor listens and helps correct recitation. This course suits learners with a basic reading foundation who need guided practice with pronunciation and the appropriate application of Tajweed.',topics=['Reading connected Quranic text with less prompting','Improving articulation through live correction','Recognising where a recitation rule applies','Developing a manageable practice routine'],path=['Read a short passage so the tutor can hear current strengths and errors.','Work on a limited set of corrections using a clear model and a repeated attempt.','Apply the correction to a different passage instead of relying only on a familiar surah.'],mistake='A faster reading pace can hide uncertainty. Focus on accuracy and understanding the correction before increasing speed or moving into detailed advanced rules.',next='advanced-tajweed'),
 dict(slug='quran-memorization-hifz',name='Quran Memorisation (Hifz)',title='Online Hifz Classes with a Sustainable Revision Plan',level='Reading foundation required',image='2026/08/course-hifz-memorization.webp',intro='Build memorisation around accurate recitation and a revision routine you can maintain. The first assessment should consider current reading, retained passages and realistic practice time alongside school, work or family responsibilities.',topics=['Setting an individual new-memorisation target','Reviewing recently learned material','Maintaining older memorisation','Noting repeated errors and revisiting them'],path=['Listen to current reading and any material already memorised.','Agree a modest new target together with specific revision work.','Review retention over time and adjust the workload before adding more material.'],mistake='New memorisation without review can create a misleading sense of progress. Ask how older passages remain in the timetable, and do not accept a guaranteed completion date before your starting point is assessed.',next='advanced-tajweed'),
 dict(slug='tafseer',name='Tafseer-ul-Quran',title='Online Tafseer Classes: Study Quranic Meaning with Guidance',level='Guided understanding',image='2026/10/qh-uae-course-tafseer.webp',intro='Explore the meaning and context of Quranic verses through guided study. Tell the tutor your reading background, preferred teaching language and whether you need an introductory explanation or a more structured sequence of study.',topics=['Understanding the passage under discussion','Distinguishing translation from an explanation of context','Learning relevant vocabulary and concepts','Bringing questions to a guided lesson'],path=['Agree the level and source material with the tutor.','Study a manageable passage and clarify the terms used in its explanation.','Review key points and bring unresolved questions to the following lesson.'],mistake='An English translation alone does not establish every contextual or legal conclusion. Ask the teacher to identify the explanation and scholarly sources being used; do not treat an isolated sentence as a ruling for every situation.',next='quranic-arabic'),
 dict(slug='advanced-tajweed',name='Advanced Tajweed',title='Advanced Tajweed Lessons for Students with a Reading Foundation',level='Advanced',image='2026/08/course-advanced-tajweed.webp',intro='Refine the details of recitation after establishing a reliable reading foundation. A trial should check whether advanced work is appropriate and identify which articulation points, letter qualities or rules need the most attention.',topics=['Revisiting makharij and articulation','Distinguishing relevant letter qualities','Applying detailed rules in actual recitation','Checking accuracy across unfamiliar examples'],path=['Assess a reading sample before selecting advanced topics.','Demonstrate a specific distinction and give time for guided attempts.','Check whether the learner applies it outside the original exercise.'],mistake='Knowing a technical rule by name is different from applying it while reading. Ask for a practical demonstration, a clear correction and a way to check the skill in another passage.',next='quran-memorization-hifz'),
 dict(slug='quranic-arabic',name='Quranic Arabic',title='Learn Quranic Arabic Online: Vocabulary and Language Foundations',level='Beginner upwards',image='2026/10/qh-uae-course-quranic-arabic.webp',intro='Study vocabulary, grammar and sentence structure encountered in the Quran. This language-focused course complements recitation; it should not be confused with a first-letter Qaida course or a complete Tafseer programme.',topics=['Recognising useful Quranic vocabulary','Understanding introductory word and sentence patterns','Connecting examples to the text being studied','Reviewing language concepts through guided practice'],path=['Explain your existing Arabic and Quran-reading experience.','Begin with a suitable vocabulary or structure exercise rather than assuming prior grammar.','Apply the concept to another example and review uncertain points with the tutor.'],mistake='Learning a word list does not automatically provide complete understanding of every verse. Keep language study connected to guided explanation and ask when a question belongs in Tafseer rather than a vocabulary lesson.',next='tafseer'),
]

AUDIENCES = {
 'kids':dict(title='Online Quran Classes for Kids in the UK',description='One-to-one Quran lessons for UK children, with a free trial, a suitable starting assessment and class times confirmed in GMT or BST.',lead='A useful children’s Quran lesson starts with readiness, not just age. Quran Hub offers live one-to-one teaching so a tutor can listen to the individual learner, introduce an appropriate reading task and explain the next step to the parent.',sections=[
 ('Online Quran classes for kids in the UK: choosing a starting point','Tell us whether your child recognises Arabic letters, reads joined words or already recites from the Quran. A trial should check the actual skill rather than place every child of the same age into the same material. Some young learners are ready for a short structured exercise; others need more time or a different format.'),
 ('What to look for in a free Quran trial for children','Watch whether instructions are clear, the child has time to respond and a correction is followed by another attempt. A quiet child is not necessarily learning, and an energetic child is not automatically unsuitable. Ask the teacher what they observed and which task should come next.'),
 ('Parent involvement without taking over the lesson','Help with the device and keep an appropriate adult available for a younger learner. Ask how parent communication and lesson supervision will work. Do not assume an academy’s safeguarding or recording arrangements from another provider’s policy; confirm Quran Hub’s arrangements directly before enrolling.'),
 ('After-school and weekend Quran classes','Choose a realistic UK-time slot with time to settle after school. Compare it with a weekend option if weekday attention is difficult. Suggested windows are planning ideas, not available appointments; teacher availability and the agreed schedule must be confirmed.'),
 ('Progress and practice between lessons','Ask for one specific task that reinforces the live lesson. Parents who do not read Arabic can still protect a calm routine and tell the teacher where the child hesitated. A useful progress conversation explains what has become more accurate, what still needs work and how the next step will be checked.')],faq=[('What age should my child start Quran lessons?','Readiness varies. Discuss attention, interest and comfort with the teacher during the trial rather than treating a fixed birthday as a guarantee that online learning will suit the child.'),('Can I request a female teacher for my child?','Yes, you can request one when enquiring. The academy will confirm whether a suitable teacher and lesson time are available.'),('Are online lessons better than an in-person madrasa?','It depends on the learner and the actual teaching options. Compare attention, correction time, routine and the child’s comfort; try the online format before deciding.')]),
 'adults':dict(title='Online Quran Classes for Adults UK',description='Private Quran lessons for UK adults starting, returning or improving recitation. Discuss your level, UK-time schedule and tutor preference in a free trial.',lead='You can begin as an adult, return after a break or work on recurring recitation mistakes without joining a children’s group. A private trial helps establish what you can read now and which course fits your immediate goal.',sections=[
 ('Online Quran classes for adults in the UK: where to begin','Describe your starting point in ordinary language. If the Arabic alphabet is unfamiliar, ask for first-letter teaching; if you can already read, bring a short sample. There is no need to pretend to know technical terminology before asking for help.'),
 ('Learning Quran online as an adult after a long break','Familiar surahs may be easier to recite than new text. Ask the tutor to check both so remembered material does not conceal a reading gap. A responsible recommendation can build on retained skills without requiring an automatic restart.'),
 ('Fitting lessons around work and family','Send a preferred UK-time window and a practical alternative. Explain changing work patterns before committing to a plan, and agree how timetable changes are handled. Flexible teaching does not by itself define refunds, credits or unlimited rescheduling.'),
 ('Reading, Tajweed, Hifz or understanding?','Choose the immediate problem first. Qaida addresses decoding, guided reading develops accuracy and fluency, Hifz requires a sustainable revision routine, and Arabic or Tafseer supports understanding in different ways. The tutor can recommend a sequence rather than asking you to study everything at once.'),
 ('Judging the teacher’s feedback','Ask for a clear model, an opportunity to try again and an explanation of the home task. A tutor who listens carefully and makes a correction understandable is more useful than a promise of instant fluency. Review the goal as your reading becomes more secure.')],faq=[('Am I too old to learn the Quran?','Adults can start at their current level. The first step depends on reading experience and the time available for regular practice, not a maximum starting age.'),('Can adult lessons be private?','The service described is live and one-to-one. Explain any tutor preference when booking and confirm the specific arrangement with the academy.'),('Can lessons be booked in UK evening time?','You can request evening or weekend windows. A teacher and recurring slot need to be confirmed before the timetable is treated as booked.')]),
 'beginners':dict(title='Online Quran Classes for Beginners in the UK',description='Find the right beginner Quran course, from Arabic letters and Noorani Qaida to supported reading, with a free one-to-one starting assessment.',lead='Beginner can mean several things: first letters, difficulty joining words, hesitant reading or a return after a long break. A useful assessment identifies the specific gap and chooses a first exercise that is achievable without rushing.',sections=[
 ('Online Quran classes for beginners: assess your starting level','You do not need a formal level label. Describe whether you recognise letters, follow vowel signs or read connected text. A teacher can then choose a short exercise and listen to how you approach it, instead of guessing from your age or the name of a book.'),
 ('Learn Noorani Qaida online before connected Quran reading','Qaida introduces the reading foundations needed before independent Quran reading. Letter recognition, sounds, vowel marks and joining are practised in a suitable order. The pace should follow secure reading, not a promised number of pages per week.'),
 ('Moving from letters to connected reading','A learner should be able to apply a skill in another example before relying on it in a longer passage. Ask how the tutor checks transfer, and tell them when a word is being recalled from memory rather than decoded from the page.'),
 ('Building pronunciation into early lessons','Corrective feedback should begin with the exercise being taught. Ask the teacher to model a sound and let you repeat it. Avoid trying to learn a large list of advanced Tajweed labels before you can hear and apply the relevant distinction.'),
 ('A workable beginner practice routine','Agree one short task, use the teacher’s model and write down questions for the next lesson. Beginners do not need a large independent workload to demonstrate commitment. A calm, repeated exercise is preferable to rushed reading that reinforces an error.')],faq=[('Do I need Arabic before starting?','No. Explain that you are a complete beginner, and the tutor can assess letter recognition and suggest an appropriate first reading exercise.'),('How long does Noorani Qaida take?','It varies with starting skills, practice, lesson frequency and individual learning needs. A completion date should not be guaranteed before assessment.'),('Is Quranic Arabic the same as learning to read the Quran?','No. Qaida and reading focus on decoding and recitation; Quranic Arabic studies vocabulary and language structure. The tutor can explain which need should be addressed first.')]),
 'female-teachers':dict(title='Female Quran Teacher Online for UK Learners',description='Request a female Quran tutor for a UK child or adult learner. Discuss English, Arabic or Urdu teaching, course level and a suitable trial time.',lead='A teacher preference should be part of the first enquiry, alongside reading level and availability. Quran Hub accepts requests for a female tutor and confirms a suitable match according to teacher and timetable availability.',sections=[
 ('Female Quran teachers in the UK: explain your preferences','Tell us whether the lesson is for a child, teenager or adult and that you are requesting a female teacher. Add the preferred teaching language and UK-time windows. A preference is not a booking until the academy confirms the match.'),
 ('Choosing an online Quran tutor: communication and qualifications','Ask the tutor to explain a small reading difficulty in the language you need. Credentials matter, but a useful trial also shows whether feedback is understandable and the learner has time to practise the correction.'),
 ('Choose a course from the learner’s level','A teacher preference does not change the reading assessment. A learner may need first-letter Qaida, supported recitation, a revision-led Hifz routine or guided understanding. Ask for the recommendation that follows the reading sample.'),
 ('Agree lesson and parent communication arrangements','For a child, confirm how an appropriate adult can remain involved, how timetable changes are discussed and where concerns should be raised. Do not infer recording, background-check or private-messaging policies from another academy’s advertising.'),
 ('Availability across the UK','Lessons are online and use UK local time. Location pages help families describe their enquiry, while the actual tutor match depends on level, language, preference and schedule. Ask about alternatives if the first requested slot is not available.')],faq=[('Is a female Quran teacher guaranteed at every time?','No. Requests are welcomed, but a suitable teacher and recurring slot need to be confirmed.'),('Can adults and children both request a female tutor?','Yes. State who the lessons are for and their starting level so a suitable assessment and teacher match can be discussed.'),('Are these women-only courses?','These are tutor-preference enquiries for the listed courses. Confirm the actual lesson arrangement and availability directly with Quran Hub.')]),
}

GUIDES = [
 ('best-online-quran-classes-uk','How to Choose the Best Online Quran Classes UK for Your Family','A practical comparison checklist for UK families: teaching quality, tutor continuity, trial lessons, fees, safety questions and progress.',[
 ('How to choose the best online Quran classes in the UK','The best fit depends on the learner’s starting point, attention, language needs and routine. An adult returning to recitation may need a different teaching style from a young child learning joined letters. Write down the main difficulty and one practical goal before comparing providers. A general ranking cannot establish who will teach your actual lesson or whether the explanation will suit you.'),
 ('Compare online Quran classes through a real trial lesson','A useful trial includes teaching, not only a conversation about packages. Listen for a clear model, a manageable correction and another opportunity to try. Ask what the tutor noticed and what should happen next. If a child is uncomfortable or cannot follow the format, that is useful information; do not treat a free trial as an obligation to enrol.'),
 ('Compare the actual teaching formats','One-to-one tuition can provide individual reading time, while a local group may offer an in-person routine and peer environment. Recorded lessons and apps can support practice but do not listen and correct in the same way as a live tutor. Compare the real options without assuming that every online class is equivalent or that every learner will prefer a screen.'),
 ('Ask about teacher continuity and communication','Find out who will teach the regular lessons, how substitutions are handled and how parents or adult learners can raise concerns. Ask to understand any qualification claims. Do not infer a DBS check, lesson recording policy or safeguarding procedure from a badge that does not explain what it means.'),
 ('Put prices on the same basis','Compare currency, lesson duration, sessions per month, course exclusions and attendance terms. A low monthly headline may represent fewer sessions or a group format. Quran Hub’s published standard plans are in USD, so a UK family should confirm the final payment amount and any payment-provider conversion before enrolling. Do not compare an advertised GBP competitor fee with a USD figure as though the currencies were identical.'),
 ('Keep the first commitment realistic','Choose a regular slot in UK local time and ask what changes when GMT and BST switch. Confirm the cancellation and rescheduling arrangement directly. A plan that fits an ordinary week is generally more useful than a demanding schedule chosen only for an unusually quiet month. Review fit through teaching experience, not a promise of guaranteed fluency or a certificate by a fixed date.')]),
 ('first-online-quran-trial','Your First Online Quran Trial: What to Ask and Observe','A parent and adult-learner checklist for preparing a useful Quran trial and making an informed decision afterwards.',[
 ('Your first online Quran class: explain your reading level','Tell the academy who the lessons are for, what the learner can currently read and what feels difficult. Include a teacher preference, teaching language and practical UK-time windows. Bring a previous book or lesson number if available. You do not need to complete a long self-assessment or diagnose every mistake before asking for a trial.'),
 ('Preparing for a free online Quran trial at home','Use the room and device planned for regular lessons. Check that the page is readable and the tutor can hear recitation clearly. A busy room, a poor microphone or repeated connection drops can change the learning experience. Resolve a basic technical issue before concluding that a child cannot learn online or before committing to a paid routine.'),
 ('Observe the teaching rather than only the performance','The learner does not need to impress the teacher. Notice how a difficult sound is explained, how long the learner has to respond and whether the teacher checks the correction in a second example. A smooth recital of memorised material alone may not show independent reading. Tell the tutor when a passage is already known by heart.'),
 ('Ask for a next-step recommendation','At the end, ask which course is appropriate, which skill comes first and what practice would support it. A clear answer can be modest. Be cautious about a guaranteed completion date offered before the tutor has heard enough reading or understood time available for practice.'),
 ('Confirm arrangements before continuing','Discuss the teacher, lesson length, frequency, currency, recurring appointment and attendance terms. For children, confirm parent involvement and the appropriate route for concerns. Keep those practical arrangements separate from the learning goal so an attractive course description does not substitute for a confirmed booking.')]),
 ('uk-quran-class-times-gmt-bst','Planning UK Quran Class Times Around GMT and BST','How to agree a clear UK-time Quran lesson schedule and avoid confusion when the clocks change.',[
 ('Online Quran class times in the UK: confirm GMT or BST','Send the academy a local time and explain that you mean the UK clock. An overseas tutor may be working from a different clock, and a calendar can display an event differently when travelling. Ask both sides to confirm the next appointment using a date and local time.'),
 ('Scheduling Quran lessons around UK clock changes','The UK normally uses GMT in winter and BST, one hour ahead, during summer. The official GOV.UK clock-change page lists the current dates. England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland share this clock pattern, although their school holiday calendars can differ. A local city page does not imply a separate city time zone.'),
 ('Leave a practical buffer before the lesson','A slot immediately after school, work or travel may not be the most productive. Consider time for a learner to settle and compare a realistic weekday window with a weekend alternative. Example slots on a website are planning suggestions and should not be treated as live appointment availability.'),
 ('Check the arrangement before a clock change','Ask whether the lesson is staying at the agreed UK local time and how the tutor will accommodate that. Check the next appointment on both calendars. Do not assume that the teacher’s overseas clock changes on the same date or that an app has understood the intended arrangement.'),
 ('Discuss travel and missed sessions separately','Travelling to another time zone may change the time at which you can participate, even when the teacher retains the agreed UK slot. Explain the dates before travelling and confirm alternatives. The possibility of joining online does not define cancellation, refund or make-up terms; those need a separate agreement.')]),
 ('quran-practice-for-children','Helping a Child Practise Quran Without Turning Home into Another Classroom','Practical ways to support a small, accurate Quran practice routine while leaving unfamiliar corrections to the tutor.',[
 ('Quran practice for kids: choose one clear task','A vague instruction to practise more leaves a family guessing. Ask the tutor for a small exercise already demonstrated in the lesson and check that the child can explain what to do. The goal might be a sound, a joined word or a short passage, rather than an entire new page.'),
 ('Supporting online Quran classes for children at home','A parent can protect a calm time, organise the device and notice where the child hesitates. If you are uncertain about Arabic pronunciation, do not guess or introduce a new rule. Note the difficulty and bring it back to the tutor so the same error is not rehearsed repeatedly.'),
 ('Notice attention and comfort','If practice becomes a daily argument, discuss the size of the task, timing and format. A tired child may need a different window; a child who cannot follow a model may need a smaller step. More pressure or a larger target does not by itself solve a learning difficulty.'),
 ('Check a skill in another example','A child can learn the order of a familiar page without applying the skill elsewhere. Ask the tutor how a correction will be checked in another suitable example. Progress should include accurate reading and less dependence on prompts, not only a growing total of completed pages.'),
 ('Review the format when needed','Online one-to-one teaching is useful for some learners, while an in-person routine suits others. Use the trial and early teaching experience to make that decision. A consistent learning relationship and an appropriate pace matter more than treating one format as automatically superior for every child.')]),
 ('hifz-revision-around-school','Building a Hifz Revision Routine Around School or Work','How to discuss new memorisation, recent revision and older review before choosing an online Hifz workload.',[
 ('Online Hifz classes: assess recitation and retained passages','A Hifz enquiry should describe current recitation and any passages already memorised. The tutor needs to hear accuracy and retention before selecting a new target. A learner beginning with several remembered surahs and a learner starting new memorisation may require different first steps.'),
 ('Quran memorisation and revision around school','Ask what will be reviewed each week and how older passages stay in the routine. Names such as sabaq, sabqi and manzil can be useful, but a label does not tell you how much time the actual plan requires. Request an explanation of the practice task in ordinary language.'),
 ('Choose a workload that survives an ordinary week','School, assessments, work and family commitments should be part of the plan. A large target that works only during a holiday may be difficult to retain later. Start with a manageable quantity and ask how the tutor will recognise that the workload needs adjusting.'),
 ('Record recurring errors for the teacher','Notice where the same error returns and bring that passage to the next lesson. Repeating from memory without correction can reinforce a mistake. A revision conversation should explain what remains accurate, what has weakened and what needs a smaller or more focused exercise.'),
 ('Keep completion promises in perspective','Memorisation rates differ, and retention is part of the goal. No short guide can guarantee a completion date. Review the plan with the tutor through accurate recitation and a sustainable routine, rather than comparing a child’s progress with someone else’s advertised timeline.')]),
 ('safe-online-quran-tutor-questions','Questions to Ask Before Choosing an Online Quran Tutor for a Child','A practical enquiry checklist covering parent involvement, teacher claims, communication, recordings and reporting concerns.',[
 ('Choosing an online Quran teacher: questions about the tutor','A website may describe an academy, while the family needs to understand the individual teaching arrangement. Ask who takes the trial, who teaches the regular class and how substitutions are discussed. Request an explanation of qualification claims rather than assuming every badge represents the same assessment.'),
 ('Online Quran lessons for kids: agree parent involvement','For a younger learner, ask how an appropriate adult can remain involved and how progress or concerns will be communicated. Agree the platform and the contact route. Do not assume that another academy’s supervision or private-messaging policy applies to Quran Hub; confirm the arrangement directly.'),
 ('Discuss recording before assuming it happens','Ask whether lessons are recorded, for what purpose, who can access a recording and whether consent is needed. A recording is not automatically a guarantee of safe or effective teaching. If a policy matters to your decision, request the current explanation before enrolling.'),
 ('Understand what happens when something is unsuitable','Find out whom to contact about communication, scheduling or the learner’s comfort. A trial is an opportunity to judge fit and ask these questions without a payment obligation. If the format or explanation does not suit the learner, discuss an alternative instead of accepting an uncomfortable arrangement.'),
 ('Keep a concise written agreement','Confirm the course, teacher preference, lesson length, timetable, currency and attendance terms. Avoid relying on broad promises such as flexible or child-friendly without discussing what they mean in practice. For urgent safety concerns, use the appropriate local safeguarding or emergency route rather than waiting for an academy marketing response.')]),
]
